# Crest: hosts Crest where Chromium draws Crest's interface, on Windows. The
# Crest window is a widget whose client area is Crest's interface, a WebUI
# (chrome://crest-desktop) that embeds each page's own surface, so pages and
# chrome share one compositor. The host compiles there, and Crest's WebUI may
# make the surface embeds Chromium allows only its WebUI browser.
#
# A Crest Browser has no BrowserView on Windows either, so the gates
# native-host.patch keeps on the Mac for that reason take Windows too:
# startup, downloads, discards and freezing, link routing and page close,
# hovered links, permission prompts, script dialogs, extension side panels,
# toasts, partial translation, tab sharing, and the features that need
# Chrome's Views frame. Gates whose hooks need interface Windows does not draw
# yet (docked DevTools, extension popups and install prompts) and the Mac's
# own (iCloud passkeys, the app bundle, the system screen picker) stay on the
# Mac.
#
# Windows hands Crest the links and documents it opens on the command line,
# and ProcessSingleton forwards a later launch's to the running Crest, so
# Crest's startup gate gives them to the host, which opens them where the
# core places them. The host also checks the default browser and installs
# signed updates, which need the installer's registration and Chromium's
# signature check.
#
# A surface embed's <embed> that loses focus to another element of the
# interface, such as a field the interface focused by script, gives the
# interface the keyboard back (crbug.com/508638062 leaves this open), so the
# command palette and the sheets take what is typed after a page had it.
#
# When the interface's renderer dies, performance_manager drops the frame
# that embeds the window's pages before their surface embeds let them go. A
# page let go after that has no embedder, so it reports no viewport
# intersection, as upstream Chromium has it after 158: the report would be a
# bad message outside any message dispatch, which ends the browser.

